      "problem": "If $f(x)={x}^{2}+2x-5$, then $f(2p-1)=$",
      "worked_solution": "To find $f(2p-1),$ we write down the function $f$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $(2p-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}+2x-5 \\\\ f(2p-1) & =(2p-1)^{2}+2(2p-1)-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2p-1) & =(2p-1)^{2}+2(2p-1)-5 \\\\  & =(2p-1)(2p-1)+4p-2-5 \\\\  & =4{p}^{2}-2p-2p+1+4p-7 \\\\  & =4{p}^{2}-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(2p-1)=4{p}^{2}-6.$",
